.TH CCMAIL 1 "2026-09-09" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccmail \- open the Gmail attachments the mailbox connector can only name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccmail \-\-auth
.br
.B ccmail \-\-status
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B ccmail \-\-search
.I query
.RI [ \-\-limit
.IR n ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B ccmail
.I message\-id
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B ccmail
.I message\-id
.B \-\-get
.I what
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.RI [ \-\-text ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Gmail connector Claude Code speaks to can search a mailbox, read a message
body, and
.I list
what is attached to a message \(em filename, MIME type, and an opaque
attachment id. It has no call that returns the attachment's
.BR bytes .
So a session can report, accurately and uselessly, that a message carries a
file called
.I Valuation.pdf
and that it cannot open it. Every question the message was actually about goes
unanswered.
.PP
.B ccmail
closes that gap. It talks to the Gmail REST API directly with its own
read\-only credential and writes each attachment to a file on disk. Once the
bytes are local, the ordinary file\-reading tools open them \(em PDFs, images,
plain text \(em so the capability lands in every session in every repo, with
nothing for those sessions to learn beyond the name of this command.
.PP
.B Attachment content is never printed.
.B ccmail
emits paths and expects the reader to go and read them. A multi\-megabyte PDF
pasted into a context window as base64 would be a worse outcome than the gap it
closes, so that is not offered even as an option.
.SH AUTHORISATION
The connector's own authorisation is held by the connector and cannot be
reached from a shell, so
.B ccmail
carries a second, deliberately narrower grant on the same mailbox: the single
scope
.IR https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly .
That grant can read the operator's own mail and nothing else. It cannot send,
reply, label, delete, or reach any other mailbox in the domain \(em including
where the operator administers that domain.
.PP
.B ccmail \-\-auth
mints it once, interactively. It needs a terminal and a browser, and it prints
the four steps to take in the Google Cloud console first: enable the Gmail API,
set up the consent screen (choose
.B Internal
for a Workspace account), create an OAuth client of type
.BR "Desktop app" ,
and copy the client ID and secret. It then opens the browser for consent on a
loopback redirect, exchanges the code, and stores the result.
.PP
A grant that is revoked, or an account that changes, shows up as an
.I invalid_grant
refusal naming that cause specifically \(em re\-run
.B \-\-auth
to mint a fresh one. No other session on the machine needs to do anything: the
credential is per\-machine, not per\-session.
.SH CREDENTIAL STORAGE
The refresh token is a real secret and is stored accordingly.
.TP
.B macOS Keychain (default)
A generic password under the service name
.IR ccmail .
It is created with
.B \-T /usr/bin/security
so that the same binary can read it back without raising an interactive
"allow access" dialog. That detail is load\-bearing rather than incidental: an
agent session has no way to click that dialog, and a credential that only works
while a human watches would not close the gap this tool exists to close.
.TP
.B A 0600 file (fallback)
Used off macOS, and whenever
.B CCMAIL_CREDENTIALS
names a path. The file holds the same JSON blob and is created mode 0600.
.PP
Either way the secret lives outside every repository. Nothing personal \(em not
the account, not a path, not the client \(em is baked into the shipped code;
all of it is resolved at runtime.
.SH SELECTING ATTACHMENTS
.B \-\-get
takes one argument describing what to fetch. The forms compose,
comma\-separated, and a hit is never listed twice:
.TP
.B all
Every attachment on the message. This is also what a bare
.B \-\-get
means.
.TP
.I n
The number shown in the listing, counting from 1.
.TP
.I word
Any filename containing that word, matched without regard to case \(em nobody
retypes an attachment name with its original capitals.
.TP
.IR pattern *
A glob over the whole filename, for example
.IR '*.pdf' .
Quote it, or the shell will expand it against the working directory first.
.PP
A selector that matches nothing is an error that lists what the message
actually carries. It is never silently widened to
.BR all .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-auth
One\-time interactive setup. Refuses to run without a terminal rather than
half\-completing.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Confirm the credential works, and report which account it reads, which scope it
holds, where the secret is stored and where downloads land. One live API call.
.TP
.BI \-\-search " query"
Find message ids using Gmail's own search syntax
.RI ( from: ,
.IR has:attachment ,
.IR newer_than:7d ,
and the rest). Present so that a session with no mailbox connector is still
self\-sufficient; where the connector is available its search is the richer
surface, and this is the fallback.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " n"
Cap
.B \-\-search
results. Default 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-get " what"
Download the selected attachments and print their paths. See
.B SELECTING ATTACHMENTS
above.
.TP
.BI \-\-dest " dir"
Where files land, overriding the default below. Refused if it falls inside a
git work tree; see
.BR "WHERE FILES LAND" .
.TP
.B \-\-text
Also write a plain\-text sidecar beside any
.IR .docx ,
.I .xlsx
or
.I .pptx
that was fetched. See
.BR "OFFICE FORMATS" .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output, never styled. Available on every mode.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-repo\-dest
Deliberately override the git\-work\-tree refusal. Exists so the guard can be
escaped on purpose rather than worked around by accident.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
One\-screen usage summary.
.SH WHERE FILES LAND
By default each message's attachments go to
.IR ~/.cache/ccmail/ <message\-id>/
.RI ( $XDG_CACHE_HOME
is honoured;
.B CCMAIL_DEST
overrides the base outright).
.PP
.B ccmail refuses to write inside a git work tree.
A mail attachment is, by definition, data that belongs to whoever sent it, and
a repository is one commit away from publication \(em the same reasoning, and
the same guard, that
.BR ccarchive (1)
puts on its archive destination.
.B \-\-allow\-repo\-dest
overrides it for the case where that really is the intent.
.PP
Filenames are taken from the message but sanitised: path separators and control
characters become underscores and leading dots are dropped, so a hostile or
merely careless attachment name cannot escape the destination directory or
arrive invisible to
.BR ls (1).
An attachment the sender left unnamed \(em common for an inline image \(em is
kept, not dropped, and named by its position and MIME type. Sometimes the
unnamed part is the scan the message was about.
.SH OFFICE FORMATS
A PDF or an image is readable the moment it is on disk. An Open XML document
.RI ( .docx ,
.IR .xlsx ,
.IR .pptx )
is a zip of XML and is not, so
.B \-\-text
unpacks one into a
.I .txt
sidecar next to the original.
.PP
This is an
.BR extraction ", not a rendering."
It recovers the words, and for a spreadsheet the cell values sheet by sheet as
tab\-separated rows; it does not reproduce layout, images, charts or
formatting. The sidecar says so in its own first line, so a reader who opens
only the extract still knows what they are holding. Where extraction fails, the
reason is reported and the original file is still written \(em a half\-answer is
never presented as the whole one.
.PP
Extraction shells out to
.BR unzip (1).
Where it is absent the original is still fetched and the sidecar is skipped
with that stated as the reason.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/ccmail\-credentials.json
The 0600 credential file, when the Keychain is not in use. Machine\-local, never
in a repository.
.TP
.IR ~/.cache/ccmail/ <message\-id>/
Default download destination.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CCMAIL_CREDENTIALS
Path to the credential file. Setting it also selects the file store over the
Keychain.
.TP
.B CCMAIL_DEST
Base directory for downloads, replacing the cache default.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Honoured when
.B CCMAIL_DEST
is unset.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up once on this machine:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccmail \-\-auth
ccmail \-\-status
.fi
.RE
.PP
List what is attached to a message whose id came from the connector, then take
the lot:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccmail <id>
ccmail <id> \-\-get all
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take just the PDFs, into a scratch directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccmail <id> \-\-get '*.pdf' \-\-dest /tmp/valuations
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find a message without the connector, then read a spreadsheet from it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccmail \-\-search 'from:accounts has:attachment newer_than:14d'
ccmail <id> \-\-get '*.xlsx' \-\-text
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested work completed.
.TP
.B 1
Not set up on this machine, the grant was refused or revoked, the message or
attachment does not exist, the selector matched nothing, or the destination was
refused. The message names which.
.SH NOTES
.B ccmail
is a capability instrument under
.IR docs/decisions/0006\-instruments\-in\-atelier.md :
its value is wholly the working relationship with Claude. The operator does not
need it \(em they open their own mail in a mail client. It exists so that the
teammate can read what the operator is already looking at.
.PP
It is also the first instrument to hold a credential to a third\-party account,
which is why the storage, the single read\-only scope and the revocation path
are documented here at length rather than left implicit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ccarchive (1),
.BR ccrepo (1),
.BR cctranscript (1)
